# Denylist, not an allowlist (see 260811-nh1-PLAN.md's trade-off matrix): a newly added
# directory that the Gradle build needs is included by default here, so this file can only ever
# make the build context slightly larger than necessary, never silently starve `./gradlew
# bootJar` of an input it reads. Do NOT exclude anything the build actually reads: gradlew,
# gradle/ (including gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar), build.gradle, settings.gradle, src/.

# Version control and agent/planning state -- never read by the build, and .claude/.planning/.gsd
# routinely hold local worktree state (including live Gradle lock files) that once caused an
# outright `docker build` failure when a concurrent process held one.
.git
.gradle
.claude
.planning
.gsd
.dev

# Build output -- regenerated fresh inside the build stage; shipping the host's stale copy in
# would be wasted context transfer at best and stale-artifact confusion at worst.
build

# Editor/IDE state -- never read by the build.
.idea
.vscode

# Repo tooling not read by the Gradle build itself.
.githooks
.github
.gitattributes

# Documentation -- large, frequently-changing, and never consulted by `./gradlew bootJar`.
docs
*.md
CLAUDE-SECURITY-*

# Credentials. build.gradle's git-hooks bootstrap block is already guarded by a
# rootProject.file('.git').exists() check, so excluding .git above makes it skip cleanly rather
# than fail -- same idea here: .env* carries DB_USER/DB_PASS and, absent this line, is swept into
# the build-stage layer by the Dockerfile's `COPY . .` (T-NH1-01). The runtime stage only copies
# the built jar, so .env never reaches the final image either way, but it still sits in an
# intermediate/cached build-stage layer without this exclusion -- a real, if modest,
# credential-hygiene win. This does NOT affect `docker compose up`: Compose reads .env from the
# HOST for variable substitution, never from the build context, so excluding it here has zero
# effect on Compose's own env-var resolution.
.env*
